  "Most students have had the experience of sitting through a lesson, nodding along, and feeling sure they understood, only to discover later that they cannot solve a single problem on their own. Psychologists call this the illusion of explanatory depth: we believe we know how something works until we are asked to describe it step by step. A bicycle is a well-known example. Asked whether they understand how one works, most adults say yes. Asked to draw the chain, the pedals and the frame in the right places, many of them produce machines that could never move.",
  "One of the most reliable ways to break this illusion is to explain what we have learned to someone else. In a series of studies, researchers asked two groups of university students to study the same short text. The first group was told that they would take a test afterward. The second was told that they would later teach the material to another student. In fact, neither group ever taught anyone; both simply took the same test. Yet the students who expected to teach organized the information more carefully and remembered more of it. {u1|Simply preparing to explain, it seems, changes the way we read.}",
